417 true] [17592186413692 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534194 17592186045417 true] [17592186413692 1071 "Consider the set $B={2,4,6}$ and the relation $R={(x,y)\\in {B}^{2}\\,:\\,x∣y}.$\nFill in the missing values in the adjacency matrix of the relation:\n\n|  | $2$ | $4$ | $6$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $1$ | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |\n| $4$ | $0$ | {{field-3}} | $0$ |\n| $6$ | {{field-4}} | $0$ | {{field-5}} |" 13194139534194 17592186045417 true] [17592186413692 1072 #uuid "774de0b5-5c02-58cd-a5ae-6ac59d12337a" 13194139534194 17592186045417 true] [17592186413692 1074 "If a relation $R$ is defined over $A={{a}_{1},{a}_{2},…,{a}_{m}}$ and $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2},…{b}_{n}}$ of cardinalities $m$ and $n,$ respectively, the *adjacency matrix* of $R$ is a $m\\times n$-matrix (table), where the element ${r}_{ij}$ at the intersection of the $i$th row and the $j$th column is given by\n\n$$\n{r}_{ij}={\\begin{aligned}1,\\,\\text{if }{a}_{i}\\,R\\,{b}_{j}, \\\\ 0,\\,\\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe relation $R$ is the set of ordered pairs $(x,y)\\in {B}^{2}$ with $x∣y.$\n\nLet's consider each row in our table.\n\n- If $x=2,$ then the values $y=2,4,6$ satisfy $x∣y.$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pairs $(2,2),$ $(2,4),$ and $(2,6),$ and we write $1$ in the intersection of the $1$st row and the $1$st column, in the intersection of the $1$st row and the $2$nd column, and in the intersection of the $1$st row and the $3$rd column in the adjacency matrix.\n\n- If $x=4,$ then the value $y=4$ satisfies $x∣y.$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pair $(4,4),$ and we write $1$ in the intersection of the $2$nd row and the $2$nd column in the adjacency matrix.\n\n- If $x=6,$ then the value $y=6$ satisfies $x∣y.$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pair $(6,6),$ and we write $1$ in the intersection of the $3$rd row and the $3$rd column in the adjacency matrix.\n\nAll the other cells in the matrix must be filled with zeros. Therefore, we have the following adjacency matrix:\n\n|  | $2$ | $4$ | $6$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $1$ | $1$ | $1$ |\n| $4$ | $0$ | $1$ | $0$ |\n| $6$ | $0$ | $0$ | $1$ |" 13194139534194 17592186045417 true] [17592186413693 1068 #edb/ref 17592186413676 13194139534194 17592186045417 true] [17592186413693 1068 #edb/ref 17592186413680 13194139534194 17592186045417 true] [17592186413693 1068 #edb/ref 17592186413691 13194139534194 17592186045417 true] [17592186413693 1069 "q-213111" 13194139534194 17592186045417 true] [17592186413693 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139534194 17592186045417 true] [17592186413693 1071 "Consider the following relation on ${R}^{2}.$\n\n$$\nR={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-1)}\n$$\n\nWhat are the domain and range of this relation?" 13194139534194 17592186045417 true] [17592186413693 1072 #uuid "8df7bc4f-b191-56e9-905d-73750dc43c25" 13194139534194 17592186045417 true] [17592186413693 1074 "Given a relation $R⊆A\\times B,$ \n\n- the *domain* of $R$ is the set containing all elements from $A$ that appear as the first component of the ordered pairs in $R,$ and\n\n- the *range* of $R$ is the set containing all elements from $B$ that appear as the second component of the ordered pairs in $R.$\n\nWith that in mind, we examine the given relation.\n\n- Notice that $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-1)$ is defined only for $x>1.$ So, the set of first components contains all real numbers greater than $1$:\n\n$$\n{x\\in R\\,:\\,x>1}\n$$\n\nHence, the domain is ${x\\in R\\,:\\,x>1}.$\n\n- Notice that $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-1)$ is continuous on $R,$ and has neither lower nor upper bounds. So, the set of second components contains all real numbers $R.$\nHence, the range is $R.$" 13194139534194 17592186045417 true] [17592186413694 1068 #edb/ref 17592186413685 13194139534194 17592186045417 true] [17592186413694 1069 "q-213094" 13194139534194 17592186045417 true] [17592186413694 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534194 17592186045417 true] [17592186413694 1071 "|  | $✠$ | $♣$ | $♠$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $✠$ | $0$ | $1$ | $0$ |\n| $♣$ | $1$ | $1$ | $0$ |\n| $♠$ | $0$ | $1$ | $1$ |\n\nThe adjacency matrix of the relation $R,$ defined on the set ${✠,♣,♠},$ is given above. Which of the following ordered pairs belong to the relation?\n\n- $(✠,♣)$\n- $(♣,♠)$\n- $(♠,✠)$" 13194139534194 17592186045417 true] [17592186413694 1072 #uuid "93d6ac86-9246-53ae-8020-58de7dcc7070" 13194139534194 17592186045417 true] [17592186413694 1074 "If a relation $R$ is defined over $A={{a}_{1},{a}_{2},…,{a}_{m}}$ and $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2},…{b}_{n}}$ of cardinalities $m$ and $n,$ respectively, the *adjacency matrix* of $R$ is a $m\\times n$-matrix (or table), where the element ${r}_{ij}$ at the intersection of the $i$th row and the $j$th column is given by\n\n$$\n{r}_{ij}={\\begin{aligned}1,\\,\\text{if }{a}_{i}\\,R\\,{b}_{j}, \\\\ 0,\\,\\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given ordered pairs.\n\n- The pair $(✠,♣)$ belongs to the relation. Indeed, the adjacency matrix has $1$ on the intersection of the $✠$-row and the $♣$-column.\n\n- The pair $(♣,♠)$ does not belong to the relation since the adjacency matrix has $0$ on the intersection of the $♣$-row and the $♠$-column.\n\n- The pair $(♠,✠)$ does not belong to the relation since the adjacency matrix has $0$ on the intersection of the $♠$-row and the $✠$-column.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139534194 17592186045417 true] [17592186413695 1068 #edb/ref 17592186413675 13194139534194 17592186045417 true] [17592186413695 1068 #edb/ref 17592186413677 13194139534194 17592186045417 true] [17592186413695 1068 #edb/ref 17592186413678 13194139534194 17592186045417 true] [17592186413695 1068 #edb/ref 17592186413682 13194139534194 17592186045417 true] [17592186413695 1068 #edb/ref 17592186413684 13194139534194 17592186045417 true] [17592186413695 1068 #edb/ref 17592186413687 13194139534194 17592186045417 true] [17592186413695 1068 #edb/ref 17592186413690 13194139534194 17592186045417 true] [17592186413695 1069 "q-213121" 13194139534194 17592186045417 true] [17592186413695 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534194 17592186045417 true] [17592186413695 1071 "Consider the sets $A={1,4,8}$ and $B={0,6,12},$ and the relation $R={(x,y)\\in A\\times B\\,:\\,x∣y}.$\nFill in the missing values in the adjacency matrix of the relation:\n\n|  | $0$ | $6$ | $12$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} | {{field-3}} |\n| $4$ | {{field-4}} | $0$ | {{field-5}} |\n| $8$ | {{field-6}} | {{field-7}} | $0$ |" 13194139534194 17592186045417 true] [17592186413695 1072 #uuid "a9e513d0-e8df-5b01-bf4e-b611860d77a0" 13194139534194 17592186045417 true] [17592186413695 1074 "If a relation $R$ is defined over $A={{a}_{1},{a}_{2},…,{a}_{m}}$ and $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2},…{b}_{n}}$ of cardinalities $m$ and $n,$ respectively, the *adjacency matrix* of $R$ is a $m\\times n$-matrix (table), where the element ${r}_{ij}$ at the intersection of the $i$th row and the $j$th column is given by\n\n$$\n{r}_{ij}={\\begin{aligned}1,\\,\\text{if }{a}_{i}\\,R\\,{b}_{j}, \\\\ 0,\\,\\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe relation $R$ is the set of ordered pairs $(x,y)\\in A\\times B$ with $x∣y.$\n\nLet's consider each row in our table.\n\n- If $x=1,$ then the values $y=0,6,12$ satisfy $x∣y.$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pairs $(1,0),$ $(1,6),$ and $(1,12),$ and we write $1$ in the intersection of the $1$st row and the $1$st column, in the intersection of the $1$st row and the $2$nd column, and in the intersection of the $1$st row and the $3$rd column in the adjacency matrix.\n\n- If $x=4,$ then the values $y=0,12$ satisfy $x∣y.$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pairs $(4,0)$ and $(4,12),$ and we write $1$ in the intersection of the $2$nd row and the $1$st column and in the intersection of the $2$nd row and the $3$rd column in the adjacency matrix.\n\n- If $x=8,$ then the value $y=0$ satisfies $x∣y.$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pair $(8,0),$ and we write $1$ in the intersection of the $3$rd row and the $1$st column in the adjacency matrix.\n\nAll the other cells in the matrix must be filled with zeros. Therefore, we have the following adjacency matrix:\n\n|  | $0$ | $6$ | $12$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $1$ | $1$ |\n| $4$ | $1$ | $0$ | $1$ |\n| $8$ | $1$ | $0$ | $0$ |" 13194139534194 17592186045417 true] [17592186413696 1068 #edb/ref 17592186413681 13194139534194 17592186045417 true] [17592186413696 1069 "q-213100" 13194139534194 17592186045417 true] [17592186413696 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534194 17592186045417 true] [17592186413696 1071 "|  | $3$ | $6$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $1$ | $0$ |\n| $4$ | $0$ | $1$ |\n| $8$ | $1$ | $1$ |\n\nThe adjacency matrix of the relation $R,$ defined over the sets ${2,4,8}$ and ${3,6},$ is given above. Which of the following ordered pairs belong to the relation?\n\n- $(4,3)$\n- $(8,6)$\n- $(2,3)$" 13194139534194 17592186045417 true] [17592186413696 1072 #uuid "e24d1221-5380-50fb-b4e2-6e139d57675a" 13194139534194 17592186045417 true] [17592186413696 1074 "If a relation $R$ is defined over $A={{a}_{1},{a}_{2},…,{a}_{m}}$ and $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2},…{b}_{n}}$ of cardinalities $m$ and $n,$ respectively, the *adjacency matrix* of $R$ is a $m\\times n$-matrix (or table), where the element ${r}_{ij}$ at the intersection of the $i$th row and the $j$th column is given by\n\n$$\n{r}_{ij}={\\begin{aligned}1,\\,\\text{if }{a}_{i}\\,R\\,{b}_{j}, \\\\ 0,\\,\\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given ordered pairs.\n\n- The pair $(4,3)$ does not belong to the relation since the adjacency matrix has $0$ on the intersection of the $4$-row and the $3$-column.\n\n- The pair $(8,6)$ belongs to the relation. Indeed, the adjacency matrix has $1$ on the intersection of the $8$-row and the $6$-column.\n\n- The pair $(2,3)$ belongs to the relation. Indeed, the adjacency matrix has $1$ on the intersection of the $2$-row and the $3$-column.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139534194 17592186045417 true]] :edb/tempids {"answer-21ad9036-c5da-521f-8faf-a4fa68cfc8c1" 17592186413636 "answer-277d0727-838a-539b-819b-da8d7ad8d022" 17592186413637 "answer-29c6da89-1bf9-5c09-a900-2173a27faf06" 17592186413638 "answer-2ce382e1-19d7-5210-abc5-29008e719eaf" 17592186413639 "answer-2d5c613d-ce6f-5d2f-9d0d-b0d5c7bb9753" 17592186413640 "answer-38955579-4bbf-53c1-b94b-4e5c238942cb" 17592186413641 "answer-3ba7b9e6-076b-57c8-a49f-c2f233d245f6" 17592186413642 "answer-4593208d-f293-53fc-9281-392ec9c6d5d5" 17592186413643 "answer-466eb5d1-24da-50af-bd41-cb8d9abdd975" 17592186413644 "answer-5995eb11-b770-5524-a8df-8a5ee4a7dcbe" 17592186413645 "answer-59e97cad-ff57-55f5-a222-de1e52ad1d9a" 17592186413646 "answer-6bff79e1-4d90-5045-88dc-0027149e07b4" 17592186413647 "answer-7cc5897b-87bf-5bd8-a43b-dcae1e66f64f" 17592186413648 "answer-7cf02d47-944c-5078-b040-436ef4d1dd79" 17592186413649 "answer-80ebed47-2563-51af-aaa3-ffac89b613bf" 17592186413650 "answer-86d8c9da-fa6b-57b8-ac61-ead9f12c077b" 17592186413651 "answer-88b74bce-a0de-5462-a031-109e23f8056b" 17592186413652 "answer-8cff8e13-d990-55d5-bfc3-2b19181defd1" 17592186413653 "answer-9316338e-177e-596d-813f-c2b271e78ea4" 17592186413654 "answer-95001e8b-9839-5f72-8653-a0fbf0785b05" 17592186413655 "answer-9e0ca2aa-12dd-5918-af70-866246d33858" 17592186413656 "answer-b580ba27-bd08-5254-8f93-b9a06915a0d8" 17592186413657 "answer-bb5492ab-8752-5496-97c7-a39039240230" 17592186413658 "answer-bf4df8e9-1089-52a8-8fc4-5cb8c93337a5" 17592186413659 "answer-bfe1f8e2-c22c-5deb-ae97-60ecf2e07b2a" 17592186413660 "answer-c65cad00-e92b-5864-82b7-3093ffea2302" 17592186413661 "answer-c6d14aef-3ac6-5e6a-ad47-cd83787d7a24" 17592186413662 "answer-c73cb8fb-53f9-5ca0-97dc-f93263818489" 17592186413663 "answer-d0194c58-f1d2-5a9f-83ca-8ef30dfdeae5" 17592186413664 "answer-d4f8bb88-c52f-5544-9279-0cef54cf414b" 17592186413665 "answer-da00a5c7-2c5d-5747-8de0-a8da60754681" 17592186413666 